--TEST--
`Core\Response::setStatus` names the last status it accepts and the first it refuses, and refuses a code that a narrowing cast would have folded back into range -- spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A bound asserted on both sides: a member that stopped one code early would
// print plausibly against either half alone. 100 is RFC 9110 § 15's first
// class and 599 its last, so these four codes are the whole of what a status
// may be.
array<uint> $spellable = [100, 101, 204, 599];
uint $set = 0;
foreach ($spellable as uint $code) {
    try {
        Core\Response::setStatus($code);
        $set = $set + 1;
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
    }
}
echo "set ", $set, " of 4\n";

// Refused: below the first class, above the last, four digits, and one with a
// trap in it. 65636 is 100 once narrowed to sixteen bits, so a member that
// narrowed with a cast rather than a checked conversion would accept it and
// answer `Continue` to a program that asked for nothing of the kind.
array<uint> $unspellable = [0, 99, 600, 999, 65636];
uint $refusedCount = 0;
foreach ($unspellable as uint $code) {
    try {
        Core\Response::setStatus($code);
    } catch (LogicError $why) {
        $refusedCount = $refusedCount + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused ", $refusedCount, " of 5\n";

// The sentence it refuses with names the code it was given rather than only
// that something was wrong, and the trap value is the one worth reading back.
try {
    Core\Response::setStatus(65636);
} catch (LogicError $tooWide) {
    echo $tooWide->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
set 4 of 4
refused 5 of 5
Core\Response::setStatus(): `65636` is not an HTTP status — a status is three digits naming one of five classes, so it is between 100 and 599
